Maybe the agent catalog is a separate concept: The allowed models for an agent are determined by the union of models from the provider (which is global) and the agent's "models" allowlist from its config. The agent's config is taken from either agents.<id> if present, or from agents.defaults if not. Since we don't have agents.main, it should use defaults. So main should have the NVIDIA models. So why the "model not allowed" error? Possibly because the user's slash command tries to spawn a new session, and that new session uses the default agent (main), but maybe the spawn request is validated against the catalog of the **default** model? Wait, the error came from our sessions_spawn call, which we made with agentId="main". That directly uses the main agent's config. It said model not allowed. That suggests the main agent's allowed models do not include moonshotai/kimi-k2.5.

But the global config's agents.defaults includes it. So either the main agent doesn't use defaults, or our config changes haven't been applied to the running gateway (despite restart). Let's check the config hash: after our last successful config.patch (the one that added NVIDIA models to defaults), the hash was cdaacc788e4687a9b11f75c17eafc2d4f24d1b001a646efc39000274dd71d960. That config had agents.defaults with NVIDIA models and primary set to moonshotai/kimi-k2.5. The gateway should be using that. Yet the spawn failed.

